  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIsolated to scattered showers are expected this afternoon into\ntonight, mainly across higher terrain areas, as an upper- level\nshortwave rotates through the region about the western \nperiphery of an eastward- departing low. With limited moisture \nand forcing for ascent, all showers are anticipated to be light \nand brief. Additionally, an overall lack of instability should \nlimit vertical growth and largely mitigate the development of \nthunderstorms. However, an isolated rumble or two cannot be \nruled out. \n\nThe passage of a second shortwave tonight into tomorrow will \nlead to lingering showers through about mid to late morning. \nLatest CAMs indicate some of these drifting into the Mid-Hudson\nValley and southwest New England, so slight chance to chance \nPoPs were added in those locations. However, by early tomorrow \nafternoon, showers will be scoured out by the departure of the \naforementioned shortwave and the building in of high pressure. \nRegionwide dry conditions will then remain in place through \nTuesday as high pressure dominates. \n\nChances for more widespread showers return Wednesday into \nThursday as a frontal system approaches overhead from the west. \nWhile the best forcing looks to stay well to our north, guidance\ncontinues to support the expectation for beneficial rain for \nareas along and north of I-90. There are medium to high chances \n(~40-80%) for at least 0.25\" of rain in these areas with low \nchances (~ 30% or less) elsewhere. At this time, there not only\nremains low confidence in severe thunderstorm potential, but the\noverall potential for thunderstorms. Probabilistic guidance is\nindicating low probabilities of thunder with deterministic\nmodels supporting that solution with very limited instability on\nboth days. However, we will keep a close eye on trends in the\ncoming days and report any changes.",
